<!-- §01 ──────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-bottleneck" data-code="bottleneck">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Quadratic Tax</div>
<h2>dLLMs Re-Encode the Prefix at Every Single Step</h2>
<p>Diffusion language models (dLLMs) like LLaDA and Dream generate text by iteratively <em>unmasking</em> tokens — starting from a fully masked response and progressively revealing predictions over T denoising steps. Unlike autoregressive models that generate one token at a time, dLLMs process the <em>entire sequence</em> — prefix plus response — at every step.</p>
<p>That's the problem: the input prefix doesn't change during generation, yet the model re-encodes it from scratch at each of the T=16 denoising steps. With a 32K token context, that's T × (L<sub>p</sub> + L<sub>d</sub>)² operations — a cost that scales <strong>quadratically with context length, paid T times over</strong>.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Recomputation Cost — Standard dLLM vs Prefilling-dLLM</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Toggle mode, then Play</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-bottleneck" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div class="viz-btns">
      <button class="viz-btn active" id="btn-std">Standard dLLM</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" id="btn-pfill">Prefilling-dLLM</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" id="btn-bot-play">▶ Play</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" id="btn-bot-reset">↺ Reset</button>
      <span class="viz-counter" id="bot-counter">step 0 / 10</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>In the animation above, each row is one denoising step. <strong>Red blocks</strong> are prefix tokens being recomputed — wasted work. <strong>Purple blocks</strong> are response tokens transitioning from [MASK] to revealed. In Prefilling-dLLM (right mode), only 2 selected chunks (teal) are accessed per step; the rest of the prefix sits dormant in the static cache.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Complexity Comparison</div>
  Standard dLLM: &nbsp;<span class="math-var">O((L<sub>p</sub> + L<sub>d</sub>)<sup>2</sup> · T)</span> &nbsp;<span class="math-comment">— full sequence at every step</span><br>
  Prefilling-dLLM: &nbsp;<span class="math-var">O(N · C<sup>2</sup> + (L<sub>d</sub><sup>2</sup> + K · C) · T)</span> &nbsp;<span class="math-comment">— prefix once, K chunks per step</span>
</div>

<p>At 32K context (L<sub>p</sub>=32K, L<sub>d</sub>=256, T=16, K=4, C=1024), the prefill cost is paid <em>once</em>, and per-step decoding cost becomes independent of prefix length. This is the source of the paper's 28× speedup — not an approximation, but an exact reformulation of what gets computed.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 ──────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-locality" data-code="locality">
<div class="section-no">02 — Attention Decays With Distance</div>
<h2>Why It Works: Response Tokens Attend Locally</h2>
<p>Why can we get away with skipping most of the prefix? Because dLLM attention is highly local. Measuring the average attention weight from response tokens to each prefix position reveals a striking pattern: <strong>attention weight drops rapidly with distance, and this decay grows stronger as denoising progresses</strong>.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Attention Decay vs Distance — Dream-7B across denoising steps</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Select a denoising stage</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-decay" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div class="viz-btns">
      <button class="viz-btn active" id="decay-all">All steps</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" id="decay-early">Early (t=1.0)</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" id="decay-mid">Mid (t=0.5)</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" id="decay-late">Late (t≈0)</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Three key observations from the paper: (i) Attention weight falls by 2–3 orders of magnitude within 500 tokens of the response. (ii) At late denoising steps (when predictions stabilize), the decay is especially severe — attending to distant prefix tokens is nearly useless. (iii) Despite the overall decay, there are quasi-periodic <em>spikes</em> at specific positions — segment boundaries that consistently absorb ~25% of the total attention mass across all layers.</p>
<p>This locality motivates two design choices: chunk-based sparse retrieval (only K chunks are "close enough" to matter), and BOS token anchors at chunk boundaries to exploit those periodic spikes (covered in §04).</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 ──────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-method" data-code="method">
<div class="section-no">03 — Cache Once, Select K Chunks</div>
<h2>The Prefilling-dLLM Pipeline</h2>
<p>The method has three stages. Crucially, the prefix is touched only once — in the prefill stage — and the per-step decode cost is bounded by K, regardless of how long the prefix is.</p>

<div class="arch-grid">
  <div class="arch-phase">
    <span class="arch-phase-label">I — Prefill</span>
    <div class="arch-box arch-box-d">Prefix (L<sub>p</sub> tokens)</div>
    <div class="arch-arrow">↓</div>
    <div class="arch-box arch-box-c">Partition into N chunks (C=1024 tokens each)</div>
    <div class="arch-arrow">↓</div>
    <div class="arch-box arch-box-c">Each chunk: intra-chunk attention → KV cache K<sub>i</sub>, V<sub>i</sub></div>
    <div class="arch-note">parallel across devices · O(N·C²) total</div>
  </div>

  <div class="arch-arrow" style="margin-top:3.2rem">→</div>

  <div class="arch-phase">
    <span class="arch-phase-label">II — Score &amp; Select</span>
    <div class="arch-box arch-box-a">Self-information score S<sub>I</sub>(c<sub>i</sub>)</div>
    <div class="arch-arrow">+</div>
    <div class="arch-box arch-box-a">Pseudo-label score S<sub>P</sub>(c<sub>i</sub>)</div>
    <div class="arch-arrow">↓</div>
    <div class="arch-box arch-box-p">Top-K chunks selected · intra-chunk top-B tokens retained</div>
    <div class="arch-note">static KV cache — reused for all T decode steps</div>
  </div>

  <div class="arch-arrow" style="margin-top:3.2rem">→</div>

  <div class="arch-phase">
    <span class="arch-phase-label">III — Decode (×T steps)</span>
    <div class="arch-box arch-box-p">Cached KV for top-K chunks</div>
    <div class="arch-arrow">+</div>
    <div class="arch-box arch-box-g">Query + response tokens (fresh each step)</div>
    <div class="arch-arrow">↓</div>
    <div class="arch-box arch-box-g">Iterative denoising — unmask [MASK] → tokens</div>
    <div class="arch-note">per-step cost O(L<sub>d</sub>² + K·C) — independent of L<sub>p</sub></div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Scoring: How do we pick the right K chunks?</h3>
<p>A key challenge is that we don't know which chunks are relevant before generation begins. The paper uses two complementary signals computed during prefill:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Self-information score S<sub>I</sub>(c<sub>i</sub>)</strong> — the negative log-likelihood of the query conditioned on chunk c<sub>i</sub>. Lower NLL means the chunk contains more query-relevant information.</li>
  <li><strong>Pseudo-label score S<sub>P</sub>(c<sub>i</sub>)</strong> — run a quick preliminary denoising pass to get tentative response tokens (pseudo-labels), then measure how well each chunk predicts them. Chunks that predict the response well are likely relevant.</li>
</ul>
<p>Combined score S(c<sub>i</sub>) = S<sub>I</sub>(c<sub>i</sub>) + S<sub>P</sub>(c<sub>i</sub>). The top-K chunks by this score are selected; their KV caches are kept and the rest are discarded.</p>

<h3>Intra-chunk token pruning</h3>
<p>Within each selected chunk, not all tokens are equally useful. The method computes a per-token eviction score — the cumulative bidirectional attention weight between each chunk token and the query — and retains only the top-B tokens (B=512 by default). This further reduces the per-step KV size while preserving the most query-relevant content.</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 ──────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-bos" data-code="bos">
<div class="section-no">04 — BOS Anchors</div>
<h2>Curing the Lost-in-the-Middle Problem</h2>
<p>Autoregressive LLMs are known to favor information at the beginning and end of long contexts, with a U-shaped accuracy dip in the middle ("lost-in-the-middle"). dLLMs exhibit a different but equally problematic pattern: with bidirectional attention, accuracy decreases <em>monotonically</em> for tokens placed farther from the response — a linearly degrading middle.</p>
<p>The root cause is an <strong>attention sink</strong>: without special tokens, the first token in the sequence attracts a disproportionate fraction of attention mass. Tokens placed in the middle are progressively ignored as the model biases toward the end (closest to the response).</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Attention Profile: Without BOS vs With Chunk-Level BOS Tokens</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Toggle to compare</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-bos" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div class="viz-btns">
      <button class="viz-btn active" id="bos-off">No BOS (standard)</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" id="bos-on">With chunk BOS tokens</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The fix is elegant: prepend a special <code>[BOS]</code> token to each chunk during prefill. These BOS tokens act as <strong>periodic attention anchors</strong> — they collect attention mass that would otherwise pile up at the sequence start, distributing it evenly across the prefix. Because every chunk has a BOS anchor, chunk scoring becomes position-invariant: a document placed in chunk 10 gets the same attention boost as a document in chunk 1.</p>
<p>Crucially, these are not attention sinks. The paper shows that BOS tokens absorb at most 12% of attention mass per layer (vs. 60%+ for a natural attention sink), and this fraction stays stable across all 28 layers. They are anchors, not drains.</p>

<h3>Before vs After: Exact-Match on Long Contexts</h3>
<p>Using a position-controlled multi-document QA task (32 documents, one containing the gold answer at varying positions), Dream-7B with YaRN scaling at 16K context drops from ~90% EM near the response to &lt;20% EM for documents in the first 20% of the context. Prefilling-dLLM maintains &gt;85% EM across <em>all positions</em> at 16K — and still achieves ~90% at 32K where vanilla Dream completely fails.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 ──────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-speed" data-code="speed">
<div class="section-no">05 — 28× Faster at 32K Contexts</div>
<h2>Speedup That Scales With Context Length</h2>
<p>The key claim is sublinear scaling: by fixing the effective context to K×B tokens (K=4 chunks × 512 tokens ≈ 2K), the per-step compute stays constant as L<sub>p</sub> grows. Every other method's cost either grows linearly or degrades from a fixed-ratio eviction policy.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Throughput Speedup vs Context Length — Dream-7B, single A800</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Lines animate on load</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-speed" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Three patterns stand out: (i) <strong>Ours grows monotonically</strong> — 9.1× at 8K, 16.1× at 16K, 28.0× at 32K — because the fixed-K budget means proportionally less compute at longer contexts. (ii) <strong>Sparse-dLLM collapses at 32K</strong> — it uses a fixed eviction ratio, so the absolute number of tokens it must process still grows with length; it's fastest at 8K but falls behind at 32K. (iii) <strong>Fast-dLLM and dKV-Cache scale modestly</strong> — they reduce per-step cost but still process the full sequence at each of the T steps.</p>
<p>The speedup at 32K is enabled by an additional attention kernel optimization: <em>Split-S FlexAttention</em>, which processes the K non-contiguous cached chunk KVs as independent splits along the batch dimension, then merges results via log-sum-exp reduction — achieving 5.8–10.2× latency reduction for the asymmetric decode attention (query length L<sub>d</sub> ≪ K×B prefix KV length).</p>
</section>

